Step 1:
We used inheritance/polymorphism by making a ThingA and a ThingB
which extends ThingA. It has all the same functionalities but 
it introduces a new concept in its movement, that is, tracking the 
iterations since last movement. We use data hiding by making all fields
private or protected (Rand), and we use encapsulation by only including
"Thing" methods in the ThingA,B classes. There is also a ThingList class
which has an implicit Node class. In this class, there are only methods
that deal with ThingLists. The driver file Lab07b.java only handles
command line input, creates the ThingList, and manages the overall program
loop.

Step 2:
Adding a new type of Thing (ThingC) was easy. I just had to make a new class
ThingC which also extended ThingA. I had to only modify the fields,
constructor, and the overridden maybeTurn method. Then in main of
the driver file I just had to initialize a new Thing and add it to 
the already declared ThingList.

Final:
Polymorphic function calls in:
Lab07b.java line  19
Lab07c.java lines 19,22

They deal with adding ThingB and ThingC to a ThingList of ThingA's.
This is possible since both of those classes extend ThingA, i.e.
ThingB and ThingC ARE ThingA's. This ensures the program works since i
can have a Thinglist of ThingA's allowing for all Thing types to be 
added, even those extended ones with additional functionalities.